BD-J: let the jar run on a Java 5 JRE

Mac OS X 10.4 and 10.5 on PowerPC top out at Apple's Java 1.5.0_19; Java 6 on
Mac was Intel-only, and the community PowerPC port of it is not something a
release should depend on. Lowering -source/-target alone is not enough: the
J2SE adaptation layer replaces JDK-internal classes, and a handful of those
changed shape between Java 5 and Java 6, so the jar would load and then die.

Measured against Apple's own 1.5.0_19 class library, the divergence is small:
java.awt.Toolkit has zero abstract methods that Java 6 dropped (it only adds),
java.awt.peer.ComponentPeer has two, FramePeer one, and java.io.FileSystem two.
requestFocus(Component,boolean,boolean,long) was already implemented, so four
methods had to be added. They are all extra methods on Java 6+, where they
override nothing and are never called, which keeps a single jar valid on both.

  - BDComponentPeer.flip(FlipContents): Java 6 reshaped it to
    flip(int,int,int,int,FlipContents). Different signature, so both coexist.
  - BDFramePeer.setIconImage(Image): replaced by WindowPeer.updateIconImages
    in Java 6.
  - BDFileSystemImpl.checkAccess(File,boolean): Java 6 changed the second
    parameter to an int.
  - BDFileSystemImpl.deleteOnExit(File): abstract in Java 5, gone in Java 6.

The last two delegate through reflection: the jar is compiled against a Java 6
rt.jar, where the delegate has neither member, and resolving them statically is
impossible. On Java 6+ the reflective lookup is never reached.

HScene is the one case that could not be solved by adding a method.
Container.getComponentZOrder and setComponentZOrder are *final* in Java 5, and
a class overriding a final method fails verification the moment it is loaded --
fatal, and unconditional, since HScene is central to BD-J menus. Both were only
ever called from inside HScene (checked across the whole BD-J tree), so they
become private helpers; anything outside now gets Container's implementation,
which is what a real Java 5 BD-J stack would have used in the first place. The
only visible difference is that Container throws on an out-of-range index where
libbluray's version returned silently.

After this, the only post-Java-5 references left in the jars are the Java 6
members reached exclusively from the dead methods above (Dialog.ModalityType,
Dialog.ModalExclusionType, FileSystem.checkAccess(File,int), setPermission,
getSpace) plus the CausedFocusEvent/FocusEvent.Cause pair upstream already
treats as lazily linked. None is reachable on a Java 5 VM.

